+++
title: Ancient Egyptian religion
hook: About 1,500 Egyptian deities are known. The religion built around them
  had its roots in Egypt's prehistory, lasted 3,500 years, and was considered
  to have fully died in the 530s.
standfirst: A complex system of polytheistic beliefs and rituals, integral to
  ancient Egyptian culture and centered on deities believed to be present and
  in control of the world. The state dedicated enormous resources to rituals
  and temples; individuals appealed to the same gods through prayer, or
  compelled them through magic.
key-facts:
  - name: What it was
    value: "A religion: the system of polytheistic beliefs and rituals of ancient
      Egypt"
  - name: How long it lasted
    value: 3,500 years, with its roots in Egypt's prehistory
  - name: Gods known
    value: About 1,500
  - name: Where it formed
    value: Land of Punt
  - name: Influenced by
    value: Puntic polytheism
  - name: Conventional end
    value: AD 537, when the temples in Philae were closed down officially
pull-quotes:
  - text: the ability to make things happen by indirect means
    attribution: James P. Allen, on the Egyptian term heka
    section: magic
  - text: eater of raw meat
    attribution: A petition from Dioscorus of Aphrodito to the governor of the
      Thebaid, dated to 567, on an unnamed man
    section: later-periods
timeline:
  - when: circa 3300 BC
    what: "The first known depictions of the fertility god Min: monumental statues
      in an ancient temple at Koptos."
    from: The first known depictions of the Egyptian god of fertility Min, appear
      in the form of monumental statues discovered in an ancient temple at
      Koptos, dated to the late Naqada II to early Naqada III periods circa
      3300 BC, and now displayed in the Ashmolean Museum following their
      discovery by Flinders Petrie in Koptos at the end of the 19th century.
  - when: around 3000 BC
    what: Egypt is unified. Some deities rise to national importance, and the cult
      of the divine pharaoh becomes the central focus of religious activity.
    from: The Early Dynastic Period began with the unification of Egypt around
      3000 BC. This event transformed Egyptian religion, as some deities rose
      to national importance and the cult of the divine pharaoh became the
      central focus of religious activity.
  - when: 2686–2181 BCE
    what: In the Old Kingdom, Ra becomes the dominant figure in the Egyptian
      pantheon and the Sun Cult takes power.
    from: The sun god Ra had been worshipped from the Early Dynastic period
      (3100–2686 BCE), but it was not until the Old Kingdom (2686–2181 BCE),
      when Ra became the dominant figure in the Egyptian pantheon, that the
      Sun Cult took power.
  - when: the 22nd century BC
    what: The Old Kingdom collapses into the disorder of the First Intermediate
      Period.
    from: In the 22nd century BC, the Old Kingdom collapsed into the disorder of
      the First Intermediate Period.
  - when: c. 2055–1650 BC
    what: Theban rulers reunify Egypt in the Middle Kingdom. Their patron god
      Montu is eclipsed by the rising popularity of Amun.
    from: Eventually, rulers from Thebes reunified the Egyptian nation in the
      Middle Kingdom (c. 2055–1650 BC). These Theban pharaohs initially
      promoted their patron god Montu to national importance, but during the
      Middle Kingdom, he was eclipsed by the rising popularity of Amun.
  - when: c. 1650–1550 BC
    what: The Middle Kingdom crumbles in the Second Intermediate Period; Theban
      rulers reunite the country and become the first pharaohs of the New
      Kingdom.
    from: The Middle Kingdom crumbled in the Second Intermediate Period (c.
      1650–1550 BC), but the country was again reunited by Theban rulers, who
      became the first pharaohs of the New Kingdom.
  - when: c. 1550–c. 1070 BC
    what: During the New Kingdom, Amun holds supreme importance; the theology of
      the period describes in particular detail his presence in and rule over
      all things.
    from: During the New Kingdom (c. 1550–c. 1070 BC), Amun held this position.
      The theology of the period described in particular detail Amun's
      presence in and rule over all things, so that he, more than any other
      deity, embodied the all-encompassing power of the divine.
  - when: c. 1070–664 BC
    what: In the political fragmentation of the Third Intermediate Period, the
      high priests of Amun at Karnak become the effective rulers of Upper
      Egypt.
    from: In the political fragmentation of the Third Intermediate Period (c.
      1070–664 BC), the high priests of Amun at Karnak even became the
      effective rulers of Upper Egypt.
  - when: 305–30 BC
    what: The Ptolemaic dynasty assumes the pharaonic role, maintaining the
      traditional religion and building or rebuilding many temples.
    from: In the 4th century BC, Egypt became a Hellenistic kingdom under the
      Ptolemaic dynasty (305–30 BC), which assumed the pharaonic role,
      maintaining the traditional religion and building or rebuilding many
      temples.
  - when: 30 BC
    what: Egypt becomes a province of the Roman Empire, the Ptolemaic kings
      replaced by distant emperors. Ptolemaic-era beliefs change little.
    from: Ptolemaic-era beliefs changed little after Egypt became a province of
      the Roman Empire in 30 BC, with the Ptolemaic kings replaced by distant
      emperors.
  - when: "452"
    what: The fifth-century historian Priscus mentions a treaty between the Roman
      commander Maximinus and the Blemmyes and Nobades which, among other
      things, ensured access to the cult image of Isis.
    from: In fact, the fifth-century historian Priscus mentions a treaty between
      the Roman commander Maximinus and the Blemmyes and Nobades in 452, which
      among other things ensured access to the cult image of Isis.
  - when: the 450s
    what: The last inscriptional evidence of an active pagan priesthood at Philae.
      On this basis Jitse Dijkstra argues that organized paganism there ended
      in the fifth century.
    from: However, its importance has recently come into question, following a
      major study by Jitse Dijkstra who argues that organized paganism at
      Philae ended in the fifth century, based on the fact that the last
      inscriptional evidence of an active pagan priesthood there dates to the
      450s.
  - when: AD 537
    what: According to the 6th-century historian Procopius, the temples in Philae
      are closed down officially by the local commander Narses the
      Persarmenian, on an order of Justinian I. The event is conventionally
      considered to mark the end of ancient Egyptian religion.
    from: According to the 6th-century historian Procopius, the temples in Philae
      was closed down officially in AD 537 by the local commander Narses the
      Persarmenian in accordance with an order of Byzantine emperor Justinian
      I. This event is conventionally considered to mark the end of ancient
      Egyptian religion.
  - when: "567"
    what: A petition from Dioscorus of Aphrodito to the governor of the Thebaid
      warns of an unnamed man alleged to have restored paganism at "the
      sanctuaries", possibly the temples at Philae.
    from: Nevertheless, some adherence to traditional religion seems to have
      survived into the sixth century, based on a petition from Dioscorus of
      Aphrodito to the governor of the Thebaid dated to 567. The letter warns
      of an unnamed man (the text calls him "eater of raw meat") who, in
      addition to plundering houses and stealing tax revenue, is alleged to
      have restored paganism at "the sanctuaries", possibly referring to the
      temples at Philae.
  - when: "1798"
    what: The French Campaign in Egypt and Syria. Westerners begin to study
      Egyptian beliefs firsthand, and Egyptian religious motifs are adopted
      into Western art.
    from: Traces of ancient beliefs remained in Egyptian folk traditions into
      modern times, but its influence on modern societies greatly increased
      with the French Campaign in Egypt and Syria in 1798 and their seeing the
      monuments and images. As a result of it, Westerners began to study
      Egyptian beliefs firsthand, and Egyptian religious motifs were adopted
      into Western art.
  - when: the 1970s
    what: "Kemetism emerges: a neopagan revival of the ancient Egyptian religion
      whose followers speak of its recreation or restoration rather than
      direct descent."
    from: Kemetism is a neopagan religion and revival of the ancient Egyptian
      religion and related expressions of religion in classical and late
      antiquity, emerging during the 1970s. Kemetics do not consider
      themselves direct descendants of the ancient Egyptian religion but
      consistently speak of its recreation or restoration.
image-plan:
  - file: Philae_temple_at_night.jpg
    role: hero
    section: lead
    caption: The first pylon and colonnade of the Temple of Isis at Philae, where
      traditional worship apparently survived at least until the 5th century.
  - file: God_Min,_Temple_of_Koptos,_circa_3300_BCE_(reconstruction_drawing_and_existing_pieces_I,_II_and_III).png
    role: full
    section: predynastic-period
    caption: "Reconstruction of the Koptos colossi of Min, about 3300 BC: three
      statues between 372 cm and 403 cm tall, and the first known depictions
      of the god."
  - file: Narmer_Palette_smiting_side.jpg
    role: full
    section: early-dynastic-period
    caption: The Narmer Palette. A woman's face with the horns and ears of a cow,
      representing Bat or Hathor, appears twice at the top; a falcon
      representing Horus appears to the right.
  - file: Pyramide_Djedkare_elevation.jpg
    role: inline
    section: old-and-middle-kingdoms
    caption: The pyramid complex of Djedkare Isesi. Pyramids with large mortuary
      temple complexes replaced mastabas as the tombs of pharaohs.
  - file: La_salle_dAkhenaton_(1356-1340_av_J.C.)_(Musée_du_Caire)_(2076972086).jpg
    role: full
    section: new-kingdom
    caption: Akhenaten and his family worshipping the Aten. Only he could worship
      the Aten; the populace directed their worship toward him.
  - file: Copper_alloy_statues.jpg
    role: inline
    section: later-periods
    caption: Copper-alloy statuettes of Anubis (left) and Horus (centre) as Roman
      officers, in contrapposto stances (National Archaeological Museum,
      Athens).
  - file: Marble_statue_of_Isis,_the_goddess_holds_a_situla_and_sistrum,_ritual_implements_used_in_her_worship,_from_117_until_138_AD,_found_at_Hadrian's_Villa_(Pantanello),_Palazzo_Nuovo,_Capitoline_Museums_(12945630725).jpg
    role: inline
    section: later-periods
    caption: A distinctively Roman Isis holding a sistrum and a situla. Her cult
      appealed even to Greeks and Romans outside Egypt, and in Hellenized form
      it spread across the empire.
  - file: Philae_Temple_of_Isis_coptic_cross_1.JPG
    role: inline
    section: later-periods
    caption: A Coptic Christian cross on a column in the ancient Egyptian Temple
      of Philae.
  - file: Th_oltar.JPG
    role: inline
    section: modern-times
    caption: An altar to Thoth kept by a Kemetic follower. Kemetics do not claim
      descent from the ancient religion, but speak of its recreation or
      restoration.
  - file: La_Tombe_de_Horemheb_cropped.jpg
    role: full
    section: deities
    caption: Osiris, Anubis and Horus in the Tomb of Horemheb (KV57) in the Valley
      of the Kings.
  - file: Casa_degli_Amorini_Dorati._Fresco._09.JPG
    role: inline
    section: deities
    caption: Anubis, Harpocrates, Isis and Serapis in an antique fresco at
      Pompeii, Italy.
  - file: Sunrise_at_Creation.jpg
    role: full
    section: cosmology
    caption: The sun rises over the circular mound of creation as goddesses pour
      out the primeval waters around it.
  - file: Nun_Raises_the_Sun.jpg
    role: inline
    section: cosmology
    caption: Nun lifts the solar barque with the new-born sun from the waters of
      creation.
  - file: Ägyptisches_Museum_Kairo_2016-03-29_Chephren_03.jpg
    role: full
    section: kingship
    caption: The Old Kingdom pharaoh Khafre embraced by Horus, the god with whom
      the king was directly identified.
  - file: Le_roi_Ramsès_parmi_les_dieux_(Louvre,_B_13).jpg
    role: inline
    section: kingship
    caption: Horus offers life to the pharaoh Ramesses II. Painted limestone, c.
      1275 BC, 19th dynasty, from the small temple built by Ramses II in
      Abydos (Louvre museum, Paris, France).
  - file: GuardianStatueofAmenemhmatII.jpg
    role: inline
    section: kingship
    caption: A figure wearing the red crown of Lower Egypt, its face apparently
      reflecting the features of the reigning king, most probably Amenemhat II
      or Senwosret II. It functioned as a divine guardian for the imiut.
  - file: The_judgement_of_the_dead_in_the_presence_of_Osiris.jpg
    role: full
    section: afterlife
    caption: The Weighing of the Heart in the Hall of Maat, from the Papyrus of
      Hunefer (19th Dynasty, c. 1300 BCE).
  - file: Maat.jpg
    role: inline
    section: afterlife
    caption: Ma'at wearing the feather of truth, against which the actions of the
      deceased were compared.
  - file: Relief_depicting_Akhenaton_and_Nefertiti_with_three_of_their_daughters_under_the_rays_of_Aton_01_(cropped).jpg
    role: full
    section: atenism
    caption: Akhenaten and Nefertiti with three of their daughters under the rays
      of the Aten.
  - file: Book_of_Gates_Barque_of_Ra_cropped.jpg
    role: full
    section: mythology
    caption: "Ra, at centre, travels through the underworld in his barque,
      accompanied by other gods: the nightly journey through the Duat."
  - file: Abydos_Tempelrelief_Sethos_I._36.JPG
    role: inline
    section: mythology
    caption: The Osiris myth in relief. Isis, in the form of a bird, copulates
      with the deceased Osiris; at either side are Horus, although he is as
      yet unborn, and Isis in human form.
  - file: El_pesado_del_corazón_en_el_Papiro_de_Hunefer.jpg
    role: full
    section: funerary-texts
    caption: A section of the Book of the Dead for the scribe Hunefer, depicting
      the Weighing of the Heart.
  - file: Re-Horakhty_Stela.jpg
    role: inline
    section: official-rituals-and-festivals
    caption: A wooden stela showing Lady Djedkhonsuiwesankh giving offerings of
      food, drink, and flowers to Re-Horakhty.
  - file: Apis_bull_on_coffin.jpg
    role: full
    section: animal-cults
    caption: The Apis bull, worshipped in Memphis as a manifestation of Ptah, held
      its position for the rest of its life.
  - file: Wedjat_(Udjat)_Eye_of_Horus_pendant.jpg
    role: inline
    section: magic
    caption: An amulet in the shape of the Eye of Horus, a common magical symbol.
      Protective amulets were worn in great numbers by ordinary Egyptians.
  - file: Opening_of_the_mouth_ceremony.jpg
    role: full
    section: funerary-practices
    caption: The Opening of the Mouth ceremony being performed before the tomb,
      intended to restore the dead person's senses and the ability to receive
      offerings.
glossary:
  - term: Ma'at
    gloss: 'A word encompassing several concepts in English, including "truth",
      "justice", and "order": the fixed, eternal order of the universe, often
      personified as a goddess.'
    from: The Egyptian conception of the universe centered on Ma'at, a word that
      encompasses several concepts in English, including "truth", "justice",
      and "order". It was the fixed, eternal order of the universe, both in
      the cosmos and in human society, and was often personified as a goddess.
  - term: Isfet
    gloss: Chaos, which the pharaohs were obligated to repel in order to maintain
      Ma'at.
    from: They acted as intermediaries between their people and the gods, and were
      obligated to sustain the gods through rituals and offerings so that they
      could maintain Ma'at, the order of the cosmos, and repel Isfet, which
      was chaos.
  - term: Duat
    gloss: A mysterious region associated with death and rebirth, that may have
      lain in the underworld or in the sky; Ra passed through it each night to
      be reborn at dawn.
    from: The Egyptians also believed in a place called the Duat, a mysterious
      region associated with death and rebirth, that may have lain in the
      underworld or in the sky. Each day, Ra traveled over the earth across
      the underside of the sky, and at night he passed through the Duat to be
      reborn at dawn.
  - term: ka
    gloss: The life-force, which left the body at the point of death and had to
      keep receiving offerings of food to endure.
    from: The elaborate beliefs about death and the afterlife reinforced the
      Egyptians theology in humans possessions a ka, or life-force, which left
      the body at the point of death. In life, the ka received its sustenance
      from food and drink, so it was believed that, to endure after death, the
      ka must continue to receive offerings of food, whose spiritual essence
      it could still consume.
  - term: ba
    gloss: The set of spiritual characteristics unique to each individual, which
      remained attached to the body after death.
    from: Each person also had a ba, the set of spiritual characteristics unique
      to each individual. Unlike the ka, the ba remained attached to the body
      after death.
  - term: akh
    gloss: What the dead lived on as, once funeral rituals had released the ba
      from the body and rejoined it with the ka.
    from: Egyptian funeral rituals were intended to release the ba from the body
      so that it could move freely, and to rejoin it with the ka so that it
      could live on as an akh.
  - term: heka
    gloss: The Egyptian term normally translated as "magic"; James P. Allen
      renders it "the ability to make things happen by indirect means".
    from: The word "magic" is normally used to translate the Egyptian term heka,
      which meant, as James P. Allen puts it, "the ability to make things
      happen by indirect means".
  - term: syncretism
    gloss: The linking of two or more different gods to form a composite deity,
      such as Amun-Ra; the links were fluid rather than a permanent merging.
    from: The relationships between deities could also be expressed in the process
      of syncretism, in which two or more different gods were linked to form a
      composite deity. This process was a recognition of the presence of one
      god "in" another when the second god took on a role belonging to the
      first. These links between deities were fluid, and did not represent the
      permanent merging of two gods into one; therefore, some gods could
      develop multiple syncretic connections.
further:
  - slug: osiris
    why: The murdered ruler at the centre of the most important of all Egyptian
      myths, who entered the underworld and became the ruler of the dead.
  - slug: duat
    why: The mysterious region of death and rebirth that Ra crossed each night,
      and that the soul had to pass through before the Weighing of the Heart.
  - slug: book-of-the-dead
    why: The best-known of the New Kingdom funerary texts, copied on papyrus and
      sold to commoners to be placed in their tombs.
anchors:
  hook: About 1,500 deities are known. [...] The religion had its roots in
    Egypt's prehistory and lasted for 3,500 years. [...] Practices such as
    mummification halted, and the ancient Egyptian religion was considered to
    have fully died in the 530s.
  standfirst: Ancient Egyptian religion was a complex system of polytheistic
    beliefs and rituals that formed an integral part of ancient Egyptian
    culture. It centered on the Egyptians' interactions with many deities
    believed to be present and in control of the world. [...] The state
    dedicated enormous resources to religious rituals and to the construction
    of temples. Individuals could interact with the gods for their own
    purposes, appealing for help through prayer or compelling the gods to act
    through magic.
